  "description": "Holder slot 8, the converting Uint32 getter, on every kind of value (QUESTIONS NG44, re-answers10). A Uint32 scalar as is; other scalars by number: signed values sign-extend (Sint32 -1 and Sint8 -1 give 0xFFFFFFFF), 64-bit values keep their low 32 bits, floats truncate toward zero through _ftol (2.9 gives 2, -1.5 gives 0xFFFFFFFF; NaN, the infinities and magnitudes of 2^63 or more give 0); Char and RZChar scalars are signed bytes, RZUnicodeChar unsigned. Arrays go through their first element's text: signed and 64-bit elements give the scalar's bits, a Float32 {1e30} prints all its digits, which wrap in 64 bits (0x19058000), a NaN element prints 1.#QNAN and parses as 1; an empty numeric array prints 0. Strings parse as numbers (12, 0x1F is 31). An empty character array, the void array and the Empty value give the empty text, which parses as 0: true with 0 (NG44: it is not false). Pointers and objects: false [choice: the original gives an address].",
